{
  "id": "d1-everyday",
  "title": "Everyday Phrases",
  "summary": "The small words you'll hear in every conversation.",
  "sections": [
    { "type": "text", "body": "<p>Spoken Arabic differs from the formal Arabic of books. Egyptian is the most widely understood dialect thanks to decades of Egyptian film and TV, so it's a good first choice. Dialect is usually written without vowel marks, so lean on the transliteration and the ▶ button.</p>" },
    { "type": "note", "body": "Sound shift to know: in Cairo, the formal <span class='ar' lang='ar' style='font-size:1.4rem'>ق</span> is usually pronounced as a catch in the throat (ʾ), so <em>qalb</em> (heart) becomes <em>ʾalb</em>. And <span class='ar' lang='ar' style='font-size:1.4rem'>ج</span> is a hard g: <em>gamīl</em> instead of <em>jamīl</em>." },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Formal vs Egyptian" },
    { "type": "table", "headers": ["Formal (MSA)", "Egyptian", "Meaning"], "arCols": [0, 1], "rows": [
      ["كَيْفَ حَالُكَ؟", "إزيك؟", "How are you? (to a man)"],
      ["مَاذَا؟", "إيه؟", "What?"],
      ["أَيْنَ؟", "فين؟", "Where?"],
      ["الْآن", "دلوقتي", "Now"]
    ] }
  ],
